1
00:00:02.068 --> 00:00:03.770
So far you have been using

2
00:00:03.770 --> 00:00:07.907
function calling to well call functions,
but function calling

3
00:00:07.907 --> 00:00:12.245
can also be used to extract structured data
from a natural language input.

4
00:00:12.645 --> 00:00:15.648
Let's dive into structured extraction.

5
00:00:15.815 --> 00:00:18.818
In the previous lessons,
we used function calling

6
00:00:18.818 --> 00:00:23.818
as a way of interfacing with tools
both internal and external.

7
00:00:24.391 --> 00:00:27.660
In this lesson, we'll actually extend
the ability of function

8
00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:31.231
calling even more to do something
called structured extraction.

9
00:00:31.598 --> 00:00:34.567
Structured extraction
is when we need to extract

10
00:00:34.567 --> 00:00:37.570
details
and insights from unstructured texts.

11
00:00:37.737 --> 00:00:41.141
For example, if we have texts like
"Mary had a little lamb

12
00:00:41.141 --> 00:00:45.011
whose fleece was white as snow"
and we wanted to attract the person

13
00:00:45.011 --> 00:00:48.815
mentioned in the text as well
as the object that's owned by the person,

14
00:00:48.815 --> 00:00:52.952
we can use function calling
as a means of extracting that information.

15
00:00:53.019 --> 00:00:56.056
The way you would do
it is we would simply provide a function

16
00:00:56.389 --> 00:01:00.660
with the person name
and the owned object as the arguments

17
00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:03.830
that the LLM needs to extract
from the unstructured text.

18
00:01:03.863 --> 00:01:08.001
Remember, this is very similar
to the behavior that the function

19
00:01:08.001 --> 00:01:12.672
calling LLM used to fill in the arguments
from the user query

20
00:01:12.872 --> 00:01:16.810
when interfacing with both Python
as well as open

21
00:01:16.810 --> 00:01:21.581
API tools in previous examples.
We will simply use that same behavior

22
00:01:21.915 --> 00:01:26.419
but here we will
just annotate the kinds of information

23
00:01:26.419 --> 00:01:29.422
we want to be extracted
and allow the other LLM

24
00:01:29.422 --> 00:01:32.425
to extract that information
from the unstructured text.

25
00:01:32.725 --> 00:01:35.161
In this example, the LLM will extract

26
00:01:35.161 --> 00:01:39.165
Mary as the person's name and lamb
as the owned object.

27
00:01:39.332 --> 00:01:42.469
Let's take a look at making
this more concrete through an example.

28
00:01:42.769 --> 00:01:45.772
Let's look at a simple example
of abstract extraction.

29
00:01:45.972 --> 00:01:49.409
You have a very small passage here
with various

30
00:01:49.409 --> 00:01:52.412
names and addresses
associated with each name.

31
00:01:52.445 --> 00:01:56.282
Suppose you want to extract the names
and map it to the addresses

32
00:01:56.282 --> 00:01:58.251
that the names are associated with.

33
00:01:58.251 --> 00:02:00.286
You can use function calling to do this.

34
00:02:00.286 --> 00:02:04.257
You will simply provide a Raven prompt
with the Function annotation,

35
00:02:04.457 --> 00:02:08.495
indicating that you want Raven
to extract the names as lists of string

36
00:02:08.761 --> 00:02:12.665
and the addresses of the names
as another list of string.

37
00:02:12.899 --> 00:02:15.735
You mentioned to Raven
that you wanted to give the names

38
00:02:15.735 --> 00:02:20.073
and the associated addresses,
and you'll provide the text from earlier.

39
00:02:21.908 --> 00:02:24.911
You can now call Raven.

40
00:02:25.044 --> 00:02:25.845
Great.

41
00:02:25.845 --> 00:02:28.681
You've called Raven with the prompt
you had earlier,

42
00:02:28.681 --> 00:02:32.552
and you've just printed it in this tool
that you've defined here.

43
00:02:33.019 --> 00:02:34.888
And the print matches what we expect.

44
00:02:34.888 --> 00:02:38.491
We have the names as well
as the associated addresses of the name.

45
00:02:39.559 --> 00:02:42.562
For more complex
ways of doing structured extraction,

46
00:02:42.695 --> 00:02:46.733
there is another method
you can use to pull out information.

47
00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:50.303
You can use data classes
to communicate to Raven

48
00:02:50.403 --> 00:02:54.107
about the different ways
you want to associate the information

49
00:02:54.107 --> 00:02:55.742
you want Raven to abstract.

50
00:02:55.742 --> 00:02:58.511
Data class is a Python class decorator.

51
00:02:58.511 --> 00:03:02.081
It signals the variables or fields
that will follow

52
00:03:02.415 --> 00:03:05.084
and define it with type annotations.

53
00:03:05.084 --> 00:03:08.855
The LLM, which has been trained on
Python, will understand this.

54
00:03:09.255 --> 00:03:12.659
And this creates a new and convenient way
to define parameters.

55
00:03:13.226 --> 00:03:18.226
See in this example you have unstructured
text yet again. Here,

56
00:03:18.565 --> 00:03:20.400
even though you have names and addresses,

57
00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:24.938
certain names are associated
with multiple addresses, such as John Tech

58
00:03:25.205 --> 00:03:30.205
being associated with 555 Silicon Street, 777
Data Drive, and 123 Digital Domain.

59
00:03:31.311 --> 00:03:34.647
The previous approach will not work
well because we have

60
00:03:34.647 --> 00:03:37.784
an unbalanced mapping of names
to addresses.

61
00:03:37.817 --> 00:03:39.752
In such more complex cases,

62
00:03:39.752 --> 00:03:42.722
you can use the data class method.

63
00:03:43.156 --> 00:03:46.993
You will simply define
a data class called record, with a name

64
00:03:46.993 --> 00:03:51.664
as one of the attributes, and a list
of strings as the address attributes.

65
00:03:52.398 --> 00:03:56.502
Keep in mind the name of the data class
isn't too important, but it is

66
00:03:56.502 --> 00:04:00.773
how Raven will understand your intentions
so it's better to keep meaningful names.

67
00:04:00.907 --> 00:04:05.907
You will define a function annotation
that dates in a list of the data class

68
00:04:05.945 --> 00:04:10.683
as the argument, and you will provide
the unbalanced text from the first cell.

69
00:04:12.285 --> 00:04:14.153
You'll run this.

70
00:04:14.153 --> 00:04:16.656
And the output will have Raven issue

71
00:04:16.656 --> 00:04:19.726
a function call, with the argument values

72
00:04:19.759 --> 00:04:23.229
being instantiations of the record
data class.

73
00:04:23.363 --> 00:04:27.300
You'll notice that some of the names
are mapped to multiple addresses,

74
00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:30.937
such as John Tech being mapped
to the three addresses we listed earlier.

75
00:04:31.004 --> 00:04:35.942
This is exactly what we wanted, and shows
a better way of extracting more complex

76
00:04:35.942 --> 00:04:39.512
insights out of your unstructured texts
using function calling.

77
00:04:39.779 --> 00:04:42.782
Another really powerful
implementation of function

78
00:04:42.782 --> 00:04:45.752
calling is in generating valid jsons.

79
00:04:45.952 --> 00:04:50.023
Sometimes it's
very hard to get small LLMs

80
00:04:50.056 --> 00:04:54.527
such as 7 or 13
LLMs to generate valid jsons.

81
00:04:54.927 --> 00:04:56.562
Function calling can help here.

82
00:04:56.562 --> 00:04:59.565
Suppose you want to generate a json
that looks like this,

83
00:04:59.899 --> 00:05:03.036
with the first level of the json
having the city name.

84
00:05:03.536 --> 00:05:07.340
The second level of the json
having information regarding the country,

85
00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:11.678
and the third level of the json having
information regarding the continent.

86
00:05:11.711 --> 00:05:16.349
You can now define tools
with the same level of hierarchy.

87
00:05:16.716 --> 00:05:20.853
Here you've defined the first tool
which contains the city info,

88
00:05:20.853 --> 00:05:25.525
which is simply a city name
with the function annotation

89
00:05:25.525 --> 00:05:29.862
indicating a location dictionary
is required as one of the arguments.

90
00:05:30.063 --> 00:05:34.200
You will define another function
called construct location dict.

91
00:05:34.701 --> 00:05:38.805
The dates in the country information
and a continent dictionary

92
00:05:38.805 --> 00:05:40.406
as one of the arguments.

93
00:05:40.406 --> 00:05:44.310
You will define a third tool
that provides the continent dictionary.

94
00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:47.880
The dates and two arguments,
each as a simple name

95
00:05:48.181 --> 00:05:50.750
and the other name. For your LLM

96
00:05:50.750 --> 00:05:52.919
to successfully call this function,

97
00:05:52.919 --> 00:05:56.656
it will need to first, construct
a dictionary for location,

98
00:05:56.656 --> 00:06:01.227
which it will do by issuing a nested call
to the location dictionary,

99
00:06:01.327 --> 00:06:06.327
which in turn, will require
a continent dictionary, which it will head

100
00:06:06.432 --> 00:06:10.737
by issuing a nested call
to the continent dictionary function.

101
00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:13.873
This will allow you to convert
the nested calls

102
00:06:14.407 --> 00:06:18.711
into the same hierarchy
that you want in your json.

103
00:06:19.479 --> 00:06:24.250
It is at this point where it's beneficial
to talk about what the locals does.

104
00:06:24.751 --> 00:06:29.751
It simply returns the current variables
defined in the current scope back

105
00:06:29.822 --> 00:06:33.860
as a dictionary with the variable name
and a variable values provided.

106
00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:35.828
Because of the way it's used here,

107
00:06:35.828 --> 00:06:39.899
it will simply return the argument values
back as a dictionary.

108
00:06:40.433 --> 00:06:41.200
Let's take a look.

109
00:06:43.970 --> 00:06:45.872
It just returns back the two arguments.

110
00:06:45.872 --> 00:06:49.175
We passed it as a dictionary,
with the argument name

111
00:06:49.175 --> 00:06:52.779
and the argument value
as being the key value pairs.

112
00:06:53.813 --> 00:06:57.183
It is at this point
where we can talk about how to use this

113
00:06:57.183 --> 00:07:00.953
approach
to convert nested calls into valid jsons.

114
00:07:01.287 --> 00:07:02.688
Let's give it a try.

115
00:07:02.688 --> 00:07:06.893
You will simply provide
the three tools you have created earlier

116
00:07:06.893 --> 00:07:10.430
in your Raven prompt,
and have a placeholder for your question.

117
00:07:11.130 --> 00:07:15.134
Here you will define your question,
saying you want the city info for London,

118
00:07:15.568 --> 00:07:20.072
which is in the United Kingdom,
which is in Europe or Afro-Eur-Asia.

119
00:07:20.339 --> 00:07:24.076
This contains the necessary information
for your json.

120
00:07:24.710 --> 00:07:28.448
However,
by passing in the tools from earlier,

121
00:07:28.748 --> 00:07:33.748
you can now force your LLM to generate
the nested hierarchy that you need.

122
00:07:34.754 --> 00:07:39.754
You will issue a call to Raven
and you will print the json. Great!

123
00:07:40.426 --> 00:07:43.963
We've gotten back a json in the exact
format that we want.

124
00:07:44.430 --> 00:07:46.499
We can now generate the valid json.

125
00:07:47.667 --> 00:07:48.367
Great.

126
00:07:48.367 --> 00:07:51.938
We now have a valid json
out of our LLMs more predictably.

